PS5LM: the causal KQ mask is built on the device that holds the KV cache.

A graph's attention mask is [n_kv, n_tokens] and llama.cpp fills it on the host
and uploads it with every batch: 21 MB for a 512-token batch at 21K of context,
over the network for an RPC worker. For a plain causal mask over one sequence
per stream only the positions are needed, so the graph gets two small inputs,
the cells' positions [n_kv] and the tokens' positions [n_tokens], and makes the
mask from them on the device (repeat, add, clamp, log: exactly 0 or -inf).
A batch that needs anything else (several sequences in a stream, a sliding
window, 2-D positions, ALiBi, non-causal attention) keeps the host mask, as
does a cache in host memory. LLAMA_KQ_MASK_HOST=1 turns it off.

On b12600 the mask is llama.cpp's llm_attn_mask, which also carries the cell
rows of flash attention over K/V rows. The device mask is one more form of it
(pos_kv, pos_tok; raw is then a node of the graph, not an input), made in
build_attn_inp_attn_mask whenever rows are not in use, and filled in
llama_kv_cache::set_input_attn_mask. So every memory whose masks come from that
builder has it: the plain cache, the hybrid one, and now the full-attention
half of a sliding-window pair (its window half keeps the host mask). Builders
that make a dense mask themselves (attention with relative positions, as
Inkling 2 uses, and the K-only caches) are as before: host mask.

